f. Common Kubernetes errors and solutions
   
   1.CrashLoopBackOff - Application crashing  -	kubectl logs --previous
   2.ImagePullBackOff - Image or registry access issue - kubectl describe pod
   3.Pending - Insufficient resources or scheduling issue - kubectl describe pod
   4.OOMKilled - Container exceeded memory limit - kubectl describe pod
   5.CreateContainerConfigError - ConfigMap or Secret issue - kubectl describe pod
   6.Node NotReady - Node or networking issue - kubectl describe node
   7.Service unavailable - Service/endpoints issue - kubectl get endpoints

5. ImagePullBackOff

      EKS application deployment is failing.

            kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
            kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o yaml
            aws ecr describe-images \
              --repository-name java-app \
              --region us-east-1
            kubectl get pods


      First, I will execute the kubectl describe pod command to investigate the Pod events and identify the image-pulling error.

      Next, I will verify the container image name, repository URL, and image tag in the Deployment configuration.

      Since the image is stored in Amazon ECR, I will also verify the IAM permissions of the EKS worker node role and ensure that it has permission to pull images from ECR.

      Finally, I will resolve the issue and verify that the Pod starts successfully.

6. What does OOMKilled mean, and how will you troubleshoot it?

    container exceeded its memory limit and was killed.
          kubectl top pod <pod-name>
          kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
          kubectl logs <pod-name> --prevous
      

      First, I will investigate the OOMKilled issue by checking the Pod's memory utilization and configured resource limits.

      I will use kubectl top pod to monitor memory consumption and kubectl describe pod to inspect the resource configuration.

      If the application requires additional memory, I will increase the memory request and limit in the Deployment YAML after checking node capacity.

      Finally, I will redeploy the application and verify that the Pod is running successfully without further restarts.

